  {
    "title": "'Putting his doubters in their place' - Rooney lands pundit of year award",
    "link": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/ck4g1l4nj85zo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
    "snippet": "Former Manchester United and England captain Wayne Rooney is named pundit of the year at the prestigious Broadcast Sport Awards.",
    "source": "BBC Sport Football",
    "feed": "BBC Sport Football",
    "published": "2026-11-06T06:22:00+00:00",
    "league": "Premier League",
    "title_key": "puttinghisdoubtersintheirplacerooneylandspunditofyearaward",
    "body": "Image source, Getty Images Image caption, Wayne Rooney has transitioned to broadcasting following a managerial career with Derby County, DC United, Birmingham City and Plymouth Argyle \"I like a lot of them artists, but I thought it was crap.\" Wayne Rooney's assessment of the 2026 World Cup final half-time show - delivered live on BBC Television - was blunt. The show, which featured Madonna, BTS, Shakira and Justin Bieber, ran to 27 minutes and 22 seconds. Rooney, who played at three World Cups for England, was unimpressed. It caught some - including fellow pundit Joe Hart - by surprise. But Rooney's plain-speaking and sense of humour through the tournament had connected with viewers. On Thursday, the former Manchester United and England captain was named pundit of the year at the Broadcast Sport Awards. , external Rooney, 41, was presented with the prestigious prize in front of 650 people from the sports television industry at a ceremony in London. \"I didn't realise how tough it was going to be when I first started doing it,\" said Rooney about transitioning from football to broadcasting. \"I've always been my own biggest critic. And even now I need to improve, I need to be better. \"It's a lot of hard work.\" Figure caption, Wayne wasn't holding back about the half-time show Rooney's move to broadcasting after a stellar playing career - followed by a testing stint as a manager - has not always been smooth. His debut as a full-time pundit on Match of the Day in August 2025 divide",
